    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Step 1: Understanding the problem\nThe problem asks for a number that is a multiple of every integer. This means that the number must be divisible by every number in the set of integers.\n\nStep 2: Identifying the lowest multiple for each integer\nTo satisfy the requirement that this number must be divisible by every integer, we need to identify the lowest multiple for each integer in the set of integers.\n\nStep 3: Determining the least common multiple (LCM)\nThe LCM of a set of integers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the integers in the set. The zero is not included in the set of integers, so we have to consider the positive integers.\n\nSince every positive integer is divisible by 1, the LCM of any non-zero set of positive integers is the LCM of those numbers when they are multiplied together. For example, the LCM of 4 and 6 is 12 because 12 is divisible by both 4 and 6.\n\nStep 4: Determining if the result is a multiple of every integer\nWhen we multiply a non-zero set of integers, the resulting number will be a multiple of every integer in that set. For example, the smallest possible positive integer is 1. When we multiply every positive multiple of 1, we get the set of all positive integers. Proving that the result of multiplying all positive integers is a multiple of all integers, we get the LCM of that set. This LCM is equal to the smallest positive multiple of all integers in the original set.\n\nStep 5: Verifying the solution\nThe smallest non-zero positive integer the result of multiplying all integers is 12, the smallest positive multiple of all integers in the set is 12. Therefore, 12 is indeed a multiple of every integer.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{12}.",
